Patricia was full of hate. If there was one thing she didn't hate it was hating things. You would honestly think the girl could make it into a sport, the amount of loathing and endless on-and-on-and-on and so forth on whatever the topic at hand happened to be. Lord help us all if you bring up a triggering topic. Today was no different, there she was, frustrated and spewing her annoyed words.

"Those fucks! Those fucks in particular with their high-fashion diets and their holier-than-thou bullshit line of smug criticism." She paused only to take a drag of her menthol cigarette. "God forbid anyone enjoy any slice of life, fuck no! No, we have to sit here and just digest their indigestible unwanted suggestions! Fuck them the hardest." Some of the girls around the table nodded, but I groaned. Patricia had clearly forgotten Rita's new found love of cleanses and hatred of anything not grown on a plant.

"You know, Patty," Rita started, already rubbing the queen of hate the wrong way with her least-favored shortening of her name. "It's likely all of your anger stems from an addiction to processed foods, additives and color dyes. You should try living chemical free for a week and see how you feel!" Her bubbliness only served to aggravate the situation even more. "You wouldn't even have to stop eating all that disgusting dead flesh, just at least cook it at home from organic!"

By this point I wanted to crawl into a hole and die. If there was anything I, Nebraska Day, hated personally it was how everyone seemed to want to be the authority on how everyone else should live their life. And unfortunately, my group of friends was no different. There is some long pause between talking - it feels like a lifetime but it was likely no more than the time needed for Patricia to take another draw of her cigarette.

"And let’s not get starting on the smokin--," Rita began.

"To fuck with you Pen," she hurled back, smashing her cigarette into the ash tray. Her chair made a squealing sound as it pushed away from the table. "To fuck with you all. I'm out."

"See ya, Pat!" I said. This sort of exit wasn't even remotely uncommon for her. That left only 5 people, including myself, at the table. Now where were we?

